/* ═══════════════════════════ ENGLISH ABSTRACT ═══════════════════════════ */
add(SEC("Abstract"));
add(P("*Background and Objectives :*  Statistical bias correction is routinely applied to global climate model precipitation before it is used in regional impact assessment. In most applications, however, the corrected output is evaluated over the same historical period that was used to estimate the correction function. Such an assessment quantifies agreement within the calibration sample but cannot demonstrate that the estimated relationship remains useful for data never used in fitting. This study therefore evaluated Quantile Delta Mapping (QDM) for bias-correcting CMIP6 daily rainfall using independent temporal validation across 12 rain-gauge stations in Prachuap Khiri Khan Province, Thailand."));
add(P("*Methodology :*  Daily rainfall observations from 12 stations and CMIP6 historical simulations from five models covering 1981-2014 were divided into a calibration period (1981-2000) and an independent validation period (2001-2014). QDM relationships were estimated exclusively from the calibration period and subsequently applied to the validation simulations without recalibration. Performance was evaluated using RMSE, MAE, MBE, PBIAS, Pearson's correlation coefficient, NSE and KGE, together with distributional measures comprising the Kolmogorov-Smirnov statistic, the Perkins skill score, quantile relative biases and Rx1day. Significance was assessed by a 2,000-replicate year-block bootstrap with Benjamini-Hochberg false discovery rate control. Individual model performance and an unweighted multi-model ensemble were assessed separately."));
add(P("*Main Results :*  Over the independent validation period, QDM improved distributional agreement in the majority of evaluation combinations: the Kolmogorov-Smirnov statistic decreased from 0.201 to 0.105 (improved in 50 of 60 evaluation combinations), the Perkins skill score increased from 0.776 to 0.859 (51 of 60), the relative bias of the 99th percentile decreased from −29.2% to −11.3%, the Rx1day relative bias decreased from −19.9% to −4.7%, and PBIAS decreased from +9.45% to −0.09%. Conversely, temporal error metrics deteriorated: monthly RMSE increased from 116.2 to 124.7 mm month^-1^ and NSE decreased from −0.711 to −0.974, with significant degradation in 32 of 60 combinations for both metrics after false discovery rate control. A pairwise decomposition of the mean square error indicated that the dispersion component was the dominant contributor to this deterioration, being the largest of the three components in 56 of 60 combinations and averaging +2,891 mm^2^ month^-2^ across combinations, against −237 mm^2^ month^-2^ for the bias component. Temporal correspondence was not materially recovered: the monthly anomaly correlation remained at 0.011 and the annual correlation increased only from 0.025 to 0.038. As a secondary finding, the unweighted multi-model ensemble attained a lower RMSE than every individual model, both before and after correction."));
add(P("*Conclusions :*  The correction relationship estimated during 1981-2000 retained a degree of temporal transferability for selected distributional properties when applied to 2001-2014, but it did not create temporal correspondence absent from the raw simulations. Distributional fidelity and time-series accuracy are therefore distinct properties, and RMSE or NSE alone should not be used to judge bias-correction quality. Validation within the historical record does not establish that the calibrated relationship remains stationary under future climate conditions."));
add(P("*Keywords :*  bias correction; quantile delta mapping; CMIP6; independent temporal validation; Prachuap Khiri Khan Province"));
add(PL("**Corresponding author*. *E-mail :* Surasit.irri@gmail.com"));

